WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.949
REMEMBER FIREFOX:
Eversince Google Chrome came out in 2008,

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:05.220
it has dominated the market.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.460
The integration with Google services and extensions
has made it the go to browser for most people.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.010
But, there are still some people who use other
browsers.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.859
Some of these choices are self explanatory.

00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:23.090
For example, the second most popular desktop
browser is Safari which is not surprising

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.119
given that it comes preinstalled on Mac.

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:30.900
The same thing can be said about Microsoft
Edge and Interent Explorer as well.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.670
But, what about Firefox?

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:39.480
Firefox currently controls 7.69% of the market
and it doesn’t come preinstalled on Windows,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.420
Mac or any mobile device.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:46.350
So, people who are using Firefox had to go
out of their way to get it and they chose

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.010
it over chrome.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:52.899
And given that billions of people around the
world use PCs, Firefox’s userbase works

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:57.079
out to a total of 362 million users.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:02.059
At first, it might be easy to write off this
segment as people who are anti big tech and

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:03.239
anti Google.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:08.540
But, if you take a closer look, you’ll see
that this isn’t just some Google hate club.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.439
In fact, there are entire subreddits that
discuss why Firefox is the best browser.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:17.740
These people aren’t using Firefox because
they don’t want to use chrome, they’re

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.120
using Firefox because they want to use Firefox.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.299
This story becomes even more interesting when
you realize how Firefox is funded.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:31.240
Firefox is a non profit organization and they
don’t runs ads or have any other internet

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.240
based services.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.759
So, they’re virtually completed funded by
donations and by far their largest donor is

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:39.470
actually Google.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:45.311
In fact, last year, Mozilla pulled in a total
of $500 million out of which $450 million

00:01:45.311 --> 00:01:46.719
came from Google.

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:51.409
Before Google got involved, Mozilla was actually
slashing their workforce and scaling back

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:52.549
their operations.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:58.049
So, in a way, Google actually saved Firefox
when they could’ve just let them die out

00:01:58.049 --> 00:01:59.409
and absorbed their marketshare.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:05.969
So, here’s why hundreds of millions people
still use Firefox and why even Google supports

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:06.969
Firefox.

00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:12.770
THE STORY OF FIREFOX:
To understand why people still use Firefox,

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:17.330
we’ll have to take a look back at why people
started using Firefox in the first place.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:23.400
Taking a look back, the roots of Firefox actually
date back to the early 1990s to a company

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.400
called Netscape.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.720
A lot of you watching may not even be familiar
with Netscape, but back in the day, they were

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.709
by far the most dominant internet browser.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:37.210
But, all of this would change when Microsoft
launched Internet Explorer.

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:41.510
Internet Explorer came bundled with Windows,
so when people bought new computers, they

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.160
just started using Internet explorer instead.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.750
Microsoft would actually end up facing an
antitrust lawsuit for this practice which

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.769
almost split up the company, but that’s
a wholenother story.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.709
Anyway, as Netscape continued to bleed marketshare
against Microsoft, they simply couldn’t

00:02:57.709 --> 00:02:59.340
keep up the fight.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.500
Microsoft had an advantage in basically every
avenue.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.269
They had more brand recognition, more trust,
more developing power, and more marketing

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:08.269
power.

00:03:08.269 --> 00:03:13.140
So, in a last ditch effort, Netscape would
try to make their browser opensource in 1998.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.080
The idea was that tens of thousands of open
source developers could help them take on

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.180
Microsoft.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.190
They code named the project Mozilla which
was a combination of the words Mosaic and

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.140
Godzilla, and they were off to the races,
or at least that’s what they hoped for.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.640
In reality, they would run into a bunch of
roadblocks.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.390
For one, the browser was rather complex for
being an opensource project which heavily

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.080
limited the number of developers who could
help out.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.970
And secondly, Netscape couldn’t even opensource
much of the browser because a lot of the code

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.080
was licensed from third parties.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.980
They fumbled around like this for several
years, but eventually in June of 2002, they

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.159
would make the hard decision of scraping everything
and starting from scratch.

00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:59.620
Initially, the project was called Mozilla
Phoenix, but this got them into trouble with

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.010
the BIOS manufacturer Phoenix Technologies.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:06.799
So, they would switch their name to Mozilla
Firebird, but this simply created confusion

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:08.569
with the database software Firebird.

00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:13.739
So, eventually, they switched their name to
Mozilla Firefox and the first official release

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:17.130
of the browser came in February of 2004.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:22.199
While Firefox faced quite a few challenges
in actually launching, the first few years

00:04:22.199 --> 00:04:24.870
after launch were actually rather smooth.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:30.661
By 2008, Firefox would control 30% of the
market, and if it wasn’t for Chrome, Firefox

00:04:30.661 --> 00:04:32.830
probably would’ve taken majority control.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.789
And the reasoning for this is quite simple.

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:40.240
Firefox was built off the success and back
of Netscape which was at one point the most

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.830
popular browser in the world.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:48.090
Firefox was essentially just a rebranded,
revamped, open source, modern reboot of Netscape.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:53.010
So, choosing to use Firefox over the rapidly
aging Internet Explorer was a rather easy

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.100
choice.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:59.490
Not to mention, the public distate for Microsoft
was rather high throughout the early 2000s

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:03.580
as they were seen as the evil monopoly that
you might associate with Google or Facebook

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.580
today.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:11.460
But, very soon, Firefox would be stopped dead
in its tracks with the launch of Chrome.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.380
CHROME TAKEOVER:
Google Chrome didn’t launch with anything

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.080
ground breaking per se.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.570
It was basically just Firefox but from Google
with a few little upgrades here and there.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:28.699
For example, they introduced the idea of sandboxing
which made individual tabs independent of

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:29.830
each other.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:34.110
This made it so that if one tab crashed, the
entire browser wouldn’t crash.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:39.750
Tweaks like this made Chrome marginally faster
and more stable than Firefox, but this wasn’t

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.270
anything that Firefox couldn’t catch up
to.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:46.860
Catching up wouldn’t really help Firefox
though because the speed and stability weren’t

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.389
really the selling factors of Chrome.

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:52.180
While they definitely made the experience
better, the real selling factors were the

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:55.010
Google ecosystem and developer support.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:59.210
Starting off with the Google ecosystem, this
is self explanatory.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.690
Chrome beautifully integrated with gmail accounts,
Google maps, Google drive, Google’s office

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.460
products, and of course Google.com itself.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:11.300
It’s kind of similar to the whole Apple
ecosystem with iCloud, iMessage, and Apple

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.300
Music.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.660
As people became more reliant on Google’s
various services, they tended switch to chrome.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:20.580
But, it wasn’t just users who preferred
chrome either.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.930
Developers were also big fans of chrome because
it was built with developers in mind.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:31.520
Chrome was built around the standards of HTML5,
and it even passed the Acid1 and Acid2 tests

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:32.870
from day one.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:36.199
If you’re not familiar with these tests,
they’re basically just a way of gauging

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:40.030
how well a particular browser can display
aspects of HTML.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:45.240
Anyway, as developers started jumping onto
the chrome bandwagon, websites became more

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.150
and more optimized for chrome.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:51.680
And pretty soon, it got to the point that
if you wanted the best browser experience,

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.490
you used chrome.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:58.360
Firefox was never that far behind, but for
the average person, there was really no reason

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.060
to ever choose Firefox over chrome.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.460
This was only made worse by Google’s marketing
power.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.190
Google didn’t even need to pay anyone to
market chrome.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:11.860
They could just advertise chrome across all
of their existing services for free, and that’s

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.240
exactly what they did.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.900
In the meantime, Firefox didn’t do much
to differentiate themselves.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:21.150
So, they basically became an opensource version
of Chrome.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.879
We should also mention that many parts of
Chrome itself were already opensource, so

00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:28.990
that wasn’t a very big advantage of Firefox
either.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.430
With all the cards stacked against Firefox,
they inevitably started losing marketshare

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.720
year after year bringing us to where we are
today.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:43.340
THE STATE OF FIREFOX:
If Chrome is so good, why do people still

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:44.340
use Firefox?

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.090
Well, there’s actually several legitimate
reasons for this starting with computational

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.460
requirements.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.650
It may not seem like it but chrome is actually
rather computationally intense especially

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.150
for being a browser.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.330
Remember how we talked about how Chrome treats
each tab separately?

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:05.080
Well, it turns out that this requires a bunch
of RAM.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.500
Firefox does this to an extent as well, but
they limit the number of processes to 6 at

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.500
a time.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.629
So, if you have 20 tabs open on Chrome, you’ll
have 20 RAM processes.

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:20.470
But, if you open 20 tabs on Firefox, you’ll
only have 6 RAM processes which is much kinder

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.160
to lower specced devices.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.830
Aside from offering better support for lower
specced devices, Firefox also offers better

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.210
support for older operating systems.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:34.800
For example, Chrome is dropping support for
Windows 7 in February of 2023 which doesn’t

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:35.800
make any sense.

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:41.240
While its been 13 years since Windows 7 was
launched, it was still the second most popular

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.220
operating system in the world as of the end
of 2020.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.890
So, you would think that Google would continue
pouring some money into Windows 7 support,

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.149
but that doesn’t seem to be the case.

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:56.220
Now, to be fair, Firefox’s extended support
also ends around the same time.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:00.570
But, if neither of them support your OS, it
makes more sense to go with the browser thats

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.380
friendlier to older computers with lower specs.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.850
Moving on from performance related benefits,
we have privacy benefits.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:10.950
It’s no secret that Google makes most of
their money by collecting your data and serving

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.760
relevant ads.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.660
Firefox on the other hand is a nonprofit that
doesn’t track your browsing history.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:21.650
By default, Firefox blocks cookies and they
generally have substantially better privacy

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:25.820
features than Google given that they don’t
have anything gain from making these policies

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:26.820
loose.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:31.709
Now, it should be noted that Firefox doesn’t
wipe out data collection altogether.

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:35.950
If you go to Google.com on Firefox, Google
can still collect a bunch of data.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:40.990
But, if you use Firefox in combination with
something like DuckDuckGo, then you actually

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.050
have a chance at protecting your privacy.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.839
But, all of this brings up the question.

00:09:45.839 --> 00:09:50.529
If Firefox is a competitor to Google and they’re
actively working to reduce how much data companies

00:09:50.529 --> 00:09:55.130
like Google can collect, why in the world
is Google funding Firefox?

00:09:55.130 --> 00:10:01.530
WHY GOOGLE FUNDS FIREFOX:
Well, the official answer is so that Google

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.019
can be the default search engine on Firefox.

00:10:04.019 --> 00:10:07.320
But, this doesn’t make a whole lot of sense.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:11.209
First of all, if a significant portion of
Firefox users are going out of their way to

00:10:11.209 --> 00:10:13.110
use Firefox over Chrome.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.930
I bet that they’re also likely to go out
of their way to use a different search engine

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:18.279
over Google.

00:10:18.279 --> 00:10:23.040
And secondly, if Google were to stop supporting
Firefox, then Firefox would likely just lose

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.060
marketshare which Google can absorb.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:29.930
So, it seems like the play for Google would
be to just pull the plug on Firefox and increase

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:31.550
their own marketshare.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.950
So, why don’t they do this?

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.339
Well, the answer is to protect their monopoly.

00:10:36.339 --> 00:10:41.040
Here’s the thing, regardless of whether
Google controls Firefox’ marketshare or

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.279
not, Chrome is already a monopoly.

00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:46.600
I mean, they control 77% of the market.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:51.840
So, either way, they’d be a monopoly, but
if they eliminated Firefox, they’d be a

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.360
monopoly at risk.

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:58.300
Right now, when antitrust regulators question
them, they have some plausible deniabliity

00:10:58.300 --> 00:10:59.769
with Firefox.

00:10:59.769 --> 00:11:03.750
They can talk about how they’re single handedly
holding up this opensource project and how

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.339
they’re encouraging competition.

00:11:05.339 --> 00:11:11.149
If they were to let Firefox die, however,
that case wouldn’t be nearly as strong.

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:15.040
At the end of the day, the initial popularity
of Firefox can be attributed to its roots

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.300
in Netscape and its superiority to Internet
Explorer.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:22.630
But, with the launch of chrome and the growth
of the Google ecosystem, people have chosen

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.839
chrome over firefox by far.

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:30.620
Nonetheless, a non negligible portion of the
population still uses firefox because it often

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:34.931
works better with older computers and operating
systems, it’s opensource, and it’s way

00:11:34.931 --> 00:11:36.530
more privacy driven.

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:41.000
And for the first time, Firefox is actually
starting to lean into its differences like

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.260
its privacy features instead of just being
an open source Chrome.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:50.800
And as people become more conscious of data
privacy, we may just see another surge in

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.089
Firefox popularity, but we’ll just have
to wait and see.

